<header><p class="enc-kicker">Religion / idea · concept</p><h1 class="enc-title">Sinicization</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as sinification, sinified</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Sinicization refers to the process by which non-Chinese peoples adopt Chinese language, culture and political practices. Accounts of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/xiongnu-empire">Xiongnu Empire</a> note that Xiongnu groups who settled within <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a> became sinicised. Some of their descendants founded states during the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sixteen-kingdoms">Sixteen Kingdoms</a> period.</p></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>His pamphlet Strategic Problems of China’s Revolutionary War (December 1936) held that China’s circumstances demanded a unique application of Marxist theory, the <b>Sinicization</b> of Marxism.</blockquote><a href="/ideology/maoism">Maoism · Origins</a></li></ul></section>
